# Proof

Let $m=AB$, $n=AC$, and let $\ell$ denote the line $BC$. Let $R=OA$ be the circumradius of $\triangle ABC$.

## Step 1. A radical-axis lemma: $P'$ is the inverse of $P$ about $A$

Since $B,B'$ both lie on the ray $\overrightarrow{AB}$, the points $A,B,B'$ are collinear; likewise $A,C,C'$ are collinear. The power of $A$ with respect to $\odot(BB'P)$ equals (signed) $AB\cdot AB' = m\cdot n$, and the power of $A$ with respect to $\odot(CC'P)$ equals $AC\cdot AC' = n\cdot m = mn$. These powers are equal, so $A$ lies on the radical axis of the two circles. The point $P$ (power $0$ to both) also lies on this radical axis, hence the radical axis is the line $AP$; consequently the second intersection $P'$ also lies on $AP$.

Applying the secant theorem from $A$ to $\odot(BB'P)$, with secants $APP'$ and $ABB'$,
$$AP\cdot AP' = AB\cdot AB' = mn.$$
As a signed product this is positive, so $P'$ is on the same ray from $A$ as $P$, and
$$\overrightarrow{AP'}=\frac{mn}{|AP|^{2}}\,\overrightarrow{AP}.$$
Thus $P'$ is the image of $P$ under the inversion $\mathcal I$ centered at $A$ with radius $\sqrt{mn}$. The identical argument with $Q$ gives
$$Q'=\mathcal I(Q).$$

## Step 2. The point $O'$

Triangle $AB'C'$ has $AB'=AC=n$, $AC'=AB=m$, and $\angle B'AC'=\angle BAC$; it is the image of $\triangle ACB$ under reflection in the angle bisector of $\angle A$ (this reflection swaps the rays $AB\leftrightarrow AC$, sending $C\mapsto B'$ and $B\mapsto C'$). Since the circumcenter of $\triangle ACB$ is $O$, the circumcenter $O'$ of $\triangle AB'C'$ is the reflection of $O$ in the angle bisector of $\angle A$. In particular
$$O'A = OA = R.$$

We claim $O'$ lies on the altitude from $A$ to $BC$. Place $A$ at the origin with the angle bisector of $\angle A$ as the $x$-axis, and write $\angle BAC=2\theta$, so
$$B=(m\cos\theta,-m\sin\theta),\qquad C=(n\cos\theta,\;n\sin\theta).$$
Solving $OA=OB=OC$ gives
$$O=\Bigl(\tfrac{m+n}{4\cos\theta},\;\tfrac{n-m}{4\sin\theta}\Bigr),\qquad 
O'=\Bigl(\tfrac{m+n}{4\cos\theta},\;\tfrac{m-n}{4\sin\theta}\Bigr).$$
The direction of $BC$ is $C-B=\bigl((n-m)\cos\theta,\,(n+m)\sin\theta\bigr)$, and
$$(C-B)\cdot O'=\tfrac{(n-m)(m+n)}4+\tfrac{(n+m)(m-n)}4=0,$$
so $AO'\perp BC$; i.e. $O'$ lies on the altitude from $A$. Moreover $|AO'|=|AO|=R$ (reflection preserves distance to $A$), and $O'$ is on the side of $A$ toward $BC$ (its coordinate along the unit normal $\hat n$ to $BC$ pointing toward $BC$ is positive, since $\hat n\cdot B=2mn\sin\theta\cos\theta>0$ and $O'$ is a positive multiple of $\hat n$). Summarizing:
$$\boxed{\;O'=R\,\hat n,\quad\text{where }\hat n\text{ is the unit normal to }BC\text{ pointing from }A\text{ toward }BC.\;}$$

## Step 3. The image of line $BC$ under $\mathcal I$

Let $d=\operatorname{dist}(A,BC)=2[ABC]/BC$. Under inversion $\mathcal I$ (center $A$, radius$^2=mn$), a line not through $A$ at signed distance $d$ with unit normal $\hat n$ maps to the circle through $A$ with center $\frac{mn}{2d}\hat n$ and radius $\frac{mn}{2d}$. Using $R=\dfrac{mn\cdot BC}{4[ABC]}$ and $d=\dfrac{2[ABC]}{BC}$,
$$\frac{mn}{2d}=\frac{mn\cdot BC}{4[ABC]}=R.$$
By Step 2 the center $\frac{mn}{2d}\hat n = R\,\hat n$ is exactly $O'$. Hence

$$\Gamma:=\mathcal I(\text{line }BC)\text{ is the circle centered at }O'\text{ of radius }R=OA.$$

## Step 4. Conjugating the reflection in $BC$

Let $\sigma$ be the reflection in line $BC$, and consider $T:=\mathcal I\circ\sigma\circ\mathcal I$ (well-defined since $\mathcal I$ is an involution). Then $T$ is an orientation-reversing involution, and it fixes exactly the points of $\mathcal I(BC)=\Gamma$ (for $T(y)=y\iff \sigma(\mathcal I y)=\mathcal I y\iff \mathcal I y\in BC\iff y\in\Gamma$). The unique orientation-reversing involution fixing a circle pointwise is the inversion in that circle, so
$$T=\text{inversion in }\Gamma\quad(\text{center }O',\text{ radius }OA).$$

## Step 5. Conclusion

Since $Q=\sigma(P)$ and $P'=\mathcal I(P)$,
$$Q'=\mathcal I(Q)=\mathcal I(\sigma(P))=\mathcal I\bigl(\sigma(\mathcal I(P'))\bigr)=T(P').$$
Thus $Q'$ is the image of $P'$ under inversion in $\Gamma$, the circle centered at $O'$ of radius $OA$. By definition of inversion in a circle:

1. $O',P',Q'$ are collinear; and
2. $O'P'\cdot O'Q' = OA^{2}$.

This proves both assertions. $\blacksquare$

